WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.020
From far away, it looks calm.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.930
A blue and white marble,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.820
turning quietly in the dark.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.650
But up close, our planet never sits still.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.000
Mud bubbles.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.210
Winds twist the air into spinning spirals.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.280
The ground cracks open.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.290
Earth was born 4.6 billion years ago,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.719
and it has been boiling,

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:26.100
freezing and reshaping itself ever since.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.391
Welcome to the wonders of planet Earth.

00:00:30.141 --> 00:00:32.051
And Earth is not alone.

00:00:32.101 --> 00:00:32.601
Every day,

00:00:32.651 --> 00:00:35.101
small lumps of rock and metal from space,

00:00:35.151 --> 00:00:36.320
called meteoroids,

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.751
slam into our atmosphere.

00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:41.061
Friction heats them until they glow.

00:00:41.111 --> 00:00:42.435
The bright streaks they leave

00:00:42.485 --> 00:00:44.501
behind are what we call meteors,

00:00:44.551 --> 00:00:45.601
shooting stars, or,

00:00:45.651 --> 00:00:48.576
when they are big and bright enough,

00:00:48.626 --> 00:00:48.982
fireballs.

00:00:49.732 --> 00:00:50.921
In November 1833,

00:00:50.971 --> 00:00:53.545
the skies over the United States put

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:55.076
on the most brilliant

00:00:55.126 --> 00:00:57.117
meteor shower ever recorded.

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:59.304
Thousands of glittering lights

00:00:59.354 --> 00:01:01.942
rained down through the night.

00:01:01.992 --> 00:01:04.392
People were woken from their sleep,

00:01:04.442 --> 00:01:06.229
and many ran outside convinced

00:01:06.279 --> 00:01:08.853
that the world was coming to an end.

00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:12.573
Sometimes, the visitor is far bigger.

00:01:12.623 --> 00:01:14.140
About 66 million years ago,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.079
a giant space rock struck near Chicxulub,

00:01:17.129 --> 00:01:19.413
in what is now Mexico.

00:01:19.463 --> 00:01:20.918
The impact hurled so much dust

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:22.311
and rock into the sky

00:01:22.361 --> 00:01:24.103
that sunlight was blocked

00:01:24.153 --> 00:01:26.826
and the climate was thrown off balance

00:01:26.876 --> 00:01:28.243
for thousands of years.

00:01:28.293 --> 00:01:30.123
Scientists believe most

00:01:30.173 --> 00:01:31.713
animal species died out,

00:01:31.763 --> 00:01:33.564
including the dinosaurs.

00:01:34.314 --> 00:01:36.074
What if it happened again?

00:01:36.124 --> 00:01:37.754
A giant meteorite would

00:01:37.804 --> 00:01:39.392
heat the air beneath it

00:01:39.442 --> 00:01:42.014
to around 60,000 degrees Celsius,

00:01:42.064 --> 00:01:44.092
ten times hotter than the surface

00:01:44.142 --> 00:01:45.134
of the Sun.

00:01:45.184 --> 00:01:47.867
It could blast a thousand cubic kilometres

00:01:47.917 --> 00:01:49.932
of rock and gas into the air,

00:01:49.982 --> 00:01:51.357
and send shock waves

00:01:51.407 --> 00:01:54.384
through the ground strong enough

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:55.530
to trigger earthquakes.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:56.080
The scars

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.074
of such impacts still mark our planet.

00:01:58.124 --> 00:01:59.974
The largest known crater,

00:02:00.024 --> 00:02:01.934
at Vredefort in South Africa,

00:02:01.984 --> 00:02:04.675
is about 300 kilometres across.

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:08.305
Not every visitor from space is destructive.

00:02:08.355 --> 00:02:10.325
Some are breathtaking.

00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:12.615
Near the North and South Poles,

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.045
the night sky sometimes fills

00:02:14.095 --> 00:02:15.490
with ghostly curtains

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.585
of green and violet light: the aurorae.

00:02:17.635 --> 00:02:21.585
They appear when streams of particles,

00:02:21.635 --> 00:02:23.935
blown from the Sun on the solar wind,

00:02:23.985 --> 00:02:26.305
collide with Earth's magnetic field

00:02:26.355 --> 00:02:28.455
and light up the upper atmosphere.

00:02:28.505 --> 00:02:30.629
Astronauts on the International Space

00:02:30.679 --> 00:02:33.106
Station can watch them ripple below.

00:02:33.856 --> 00:02:35.616
Then there are comets:

00:02:35.666 --> 00:02:36.366
chunks of ice,

00:02:36.416 --> 00:02:38.499
rock and frozen gas that blaze

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:41.456
with light as they approach the Sun.

00:02:41.506 --> 00:02:43.276
In 1996, Comet Hyakutake passed

00:02:43.326 --> 00:02:45.896
just 15 million kilometres from Earth

00:02:45.946 --> 00:02:46.891
and became one

00:02:46.941 --> 00:02:48.882
of the brightest comets seen

00:02:48.932 --> 00:02:50.376
in two hundred years.

00:02:50.426 --> 00:02:51.471
It is now heading back

00:02:51.521 --> 00:02:54.736
to the edge of the Solar System,

00:02:54.786 --> 00:02:57.256
and no one alive today will see it again.

00:02:57.306 --> 00:02:58.412
It will not return

00:02:58.462 --> 00:03:00.617
for tens of thousands of years.

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:04.882
To understand why our planet shakes

00:03:04.932 --> 00:03:07.837
and splits, we need to travel to its heart.

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.447
At the centre of the Earth,

00:03:09.497 --> 00:03:11.431
the temperature reaches

00:03:11.481 --> 00:03:13.187
about 5,400 degrees Celsius,

00:03:13.237 --> 00:03:15.637
as hot as the surface of the Sun.

00:03:15.687 --> 00:03:17.647
Like a giant engine room,

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.037
this heat drives slow,

00:03:19.087 --> 00:03:21.067
massive movements of rock

00:03:21.117 --> 00:03:23.601
that crack the planet's outer shell

00:03:23.651 --> 00:03:24.108
into pieces.

00:03:24.858 --> 00:03:26.688
Earth is built in layers.

00:03:26.738 --> 00:03:28.955
At the centre sits a solid inner core

00:03:29.005 --> 00:03:30.398
of iron and nickel.

00:03:30.448 --> 00:03:32.828
Around it swirls a liquid outer core.

00:03:32.878 --> 00:03:34.668
Above that lies the mantle,

00:03:34.718 --> 00:03:36.180
a thick layer of hot rock

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.928
that flows very slowly,

00:03:37.978 --> 00:03:39.618
like extremely thick syrup.

00:03:39.668 --> 00:03:41.064
And on top is the thin,

00:03:41.114 --> 00:03:42.848
rocky crust we live on.

00:03:42.898 --> 00:03:44.008
As the mantle churns,

00:03:44.058 --> 00:03:45.279
it pulls and pushes

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:47.219
on the crust until it cracks.

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:51.300
Those cracks divide the surface into giant,

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.859
rigid slabs called tectonic plates.

00:03:53.909 --> 00:03:56.979
The North American, Eurasian, African,

00:03:57.029 --> 00:04:00.789
South American, Pacific, Australian, Indian,

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:01.339
Arabian,

00:04:01.389 --> 00:04:03.829
Nazca and Philippine plates all drift

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:05.779
across the mantle,

00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:08.029
a few centimetres every year.

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:10.829
Where they meet, collide or pull apart,

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:12.690
the Earth gets dramatic.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.252
Nowhere more

00:04:14.302 --> 00:04:16.730
so than around the Pacific Ocean.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:17.988
This vast horseshoe

00:04:18.038 --> 00:04:21.390
of plate boundaries is called the Ring

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:21.940
of Fire.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:23.398
It holds more than 75 percent

00:04:23.448 --> 00:04:25.440
of the world's volcanoes,

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:27.402
and it is the most violent zone

00:04:27.452 --> 00:04:29.270
of earthquakes on the planet.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:30.500
The Andes, Mount St.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.290
Helens and the islands

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.497
of Japan were all shaped

00:04:34.547 --> 00:04:35.881
by its restless energy.

00:04:36.631 --> 00:04:38.701
Some plates are moving apart.

00:04:38.751 --> 00:04:41.333
Hidden beneath the Atlantic Ocean lies one

00:04:41.383 --> 00:04:43.531
of the longest mountain chains on Earth:

00:04:43.581 --> 00:04:45.151
the Mid-Atlantic Ridge,

00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:47.821
stretching about 16,000 kilometres.

00:04:47.871 --> 00:04:50.401
Here, hot mantle rock rises,

00:04:50.451 --> 00:04:52.311
lava seeps up through the gap,

00:04:52.361 --> 00:04:54.171
and new seafloor is created.

00:04:54.221 --> 00:04:56.441
The plates are forced further apart,

00:04:56.491 --> 00:04:57.931
and the Atlantic Ocean grows

00:04:57.981 --> 00:04:59.672
a little wider every year.

00:05:00.421 --> 00:05:01.274
In one place,

00:05:01.324 --> 00:05:03.712
the ridge rises above the waves.

00:05:03.761 --> 00:05:06.067
Iceland is an island of fire and ice,

00:05:06.117 --> 00:05:08.692
largely covered by glaciers,

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.199
yet built entirely by volcanoes

00:05:10.249 --> 00:05:12.642
on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.

00:05:12.691 --> 00:05:14.889
Huge cracks and rifts run across the land,

00:05:14.939 --> 00:05:17.389
tracing the line where North America

00:05:17.439 --> 00:05:19.751
and Europe are slowly pulling away

00:05:19.801 --> 00:05:20.862
from each other.

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:22.121
In 2010,

00:05:22.171 --> 00:05:24.914
one Icelandic volcano reminded the world

00:05:24.964 --> 00:05:25.891
of that power.

00:05:25.941 --> 00:05:28.152
Eyjafjallajökull blasted a plume

00:05:28.202 --> 00:05:30.219
of ash more than 11 kilometres

00:05:30.269 --> 00:05:31.127
into the sky.

00:05:31.177 --> 00:05:32.402
Winds carried the cloud

00:05:32.452 --> 00:05:35.525
across the North Atlantic toward Britain

00:05:35.575 --> 00:05:36.293
and Europe,

00:05:36.343 --> 00:05:39.582
and airports across the continent closed.

00:05:39.632 --> 00:05:40.413
For about a week,

00:05:40.463 --> 00:05:42.576
much of Europe's air travel came

00:05:42.626 --> 00:05:43.693
to a standstill.

00:05:45.543 --> 00:05:47.723
When India crashed into Asia,

00:05:47.773 --> 00:05:49.120
the collision pushed up

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.083
the greatest mountain range on land:

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:52.283
the Himalayas.

00:05:52.333 --> 00:05:54.553
In geological terms they are young,

00:05:54.603 --> 00:05:57.903
just 50 million years old, and still rising.

00:05:57.953 --> 00:06:01.203
At their crown stands the highest mountain

00:06:01.253 --> 00:06:01.753
on Earth.

00:06:01.803 --> 00:06:03.703
In Tibet it is called Chomolungma.

00:06:03.753 --> 00:06:05.753
In Nepal, Sagarmatha.

00:06:05.803 --> 00:06:07.599
The rest of the world knows it

00:06:07.649 --> 00:06:08.784
as Mount Everest.

00:06:09.534 --> 00:06:12.474
Its modern story began with surveyors.

00:06:12.524 --> 00:06:14.034
In 1841, the British survey

00:06:14.084 --> 00:06:17.024
of India recorded the position

00:06:17.074 --> 00:06:19.484
of a remote summit known simply

00:06:19.534 --> 00:06:20.314
by a letter.

00:06:20.364 --> 00:06:23.770
In 1852, calculations showed it was taller

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.384
than any mountain ever measured.

00:06:26.434 --> 00:06:26.944
In 1865,

00:06:26.994 --> 00:06:30.704
it was renamed after Sir George Everest,

00:06:30.754 --> 00:06:33.055
the former Surveyor General of India.

00:06:33.805 --> 00:06:35.750
Measuring the exact height

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.505
of Everest has caused years of debate.

00:06:38.555 --> 00:06:41.525
Snow, ice and different survey

00:06:41.575 --> 00:06:43.271
methods all change the answer.

00:06:43.321 --> 00:06:43.885
In 2020,

00:06:43.935 --> 00:06:47.031
China and Nepal announced a joint figure:

00:06:47.081 --> 00:06:49.486
8,848.86 metres above sea level.

00:06:50.236 --> 00:06:51.036
For decades,

00:06:51.086 --> 00:06:53.036
the summit seemed impossible.

00:06:53.086 --> 00:06:55.708
In 1924, a British expedition climbed

00:06:55.758 --> 00:06:58.681
to about 8,570 metres before turning back.

00:06:58.731 --> 00:06:59.722
Then, on 29 May 1953,

00:06:59.772 --> 00:07:02.556
Edmund Hillary of New Zealand

00:07:02.606 --> 00:07:03.495
and Tenzing Norgay

00:07:03.545 --> 00:07:05.866
of Nepal became the first people

00:07:05.916 --> 00:07:07.813
to stand on top of the world.

00:07:07.863 --> 00:07:10.866
Since then, thousands have followed,

00:07:10.916 --> 00:07:12.344
including a retired Japanese

00:07:12.394 --> 00:07:14.467
teacher who reached the summit

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:16.237
in 2007 at the age of 71.

00:07:16.987 --> 00:07:18.846
The most popular route starts

00:07:18.896 --> 00:07:21.407
in Nepal and climbs the Southeast Ridge.

00:07:21.457 --> 00:07:22.334
At Base Camp,

00:07:22.384 --> 00:07:25.087
on the Khumbu Glacier at 5,400 metres,

00:07:25.137 --> 00:07:26.152
climbers spend weeks

00:07:26.202 --> 00:07:27.929
letting their bodies adjust

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:28.847
to the thin air.

00:07:28.897 --> 00:07:30.233
Just above lies one

00:07:30.283 --> 00:07:32.487
of the deadliest sections of the climb:

00:07:32.537 --> 00:07:34.067
the Khumbu Icefall,

00:07:34.117 --> 00:07:35.959
a maze of shifting ice towers

00:07:36.009 --> 00:07:37.377
and deep crevasses,

00:07:37.427 --> 00:07:39.908
crossed with ropes and aluminium ladders.

00:07:40.658 --> 00:07:42.038
Above the icefall,

00:07:42.088 --> 00:07:44.508
climbers move up through a chain

00:07:44.558 --> 00:07:46.968
of camps: Camp 1 at about 6,000 metres,

00:07:47.018 --> 00:07:50.373
Camp 2 at 6,500, Camp 3 at 7,500,

00:07:50.423 --> 00:07:52.534
and Camp 4 at 8,000 metres.

00:07:52.584 --> 00:07:55.416
They scramble with ropes over a band

00:07:55.466 --> 00:07:58.668
of black rock called the Geneva Spur,

00:07:58.718 --> 00:08:00.638
surrounded by giant neighbours:

00:08:00.688 --> 00:08:02.299
Lhotse and Nuptse.

00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:06.429
Above 8,000 metres lies the Death Zone.

00:08:06.479 --> 00:08:08.334
Here, each breath holds only a third

00:08:08.384 --> 00:08:10.749
of the oxygen found at sea level,

00:08:10.799 --> 00:08:13.569
and the cold can freeze skin in minutes.

00:08:13.619 --> 00:08:16.669
The human body cannot survive here

00:08:16.719 --> 00:08:17.219
for long.

00:08:17.269 --> 00:08:19.469
Climbers use ice axes and crampons,

00:08:19.519 --> 00:08:21.139
metal spikes on their boots,

00:08:21.189 --> 00:08:22.774
to cling to walls of ice

00:08:22.824 --> 00:08:25.050
on the final push to the summit.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:29.270
Sadly, Everest now holds another title:

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.830
the world's highest garbage dump.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:35.210
Climbers and tourists have

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:38.200
left behind plastic bottles,

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.715
food packaging and empty oxygen tanks.

00:08:40.765 --> 00:08:44.600
And Everest is not alone up there.

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:48.180
The five tallest peaks on Earth are Everest,

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.250
K2 at 8,611 metres,

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:52.990
Kangchenjunga at 8,586,

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.001
Lhotse at 8,516 and Makalu at 8,485.

00:08:57.851 --> 00:08:59.791
The ground splits open.

00:08:59.841 --> 00:09:02.579
Molten rock, ash and toxic gas burst out

00:09:02.629 --> 00:09:04.399
with unimaginable force.

00:09:04.449 --> 00:09:06.749
A volcanic eruption is a window

00:09:06.799 --> 00:09:07.735
into the heat

00:09:07.785 --> 00:09:11.581
and pressure hidden far beneath our feet.

00:09:11.631 --> 00:09:12.908
Scientists rate eruptions

00:09:12.958 --> 00:09:16.031
on the Volcanic Explosivity Index,

00:09:16.081 --> 00:09:16.934
a scale that runs

00:09:16.984 --> 00:09:18.731
from "gentle" all the way up

00:09:18.781 --> 00:09:20.172
to "mega-colossal".

00:09:20.922 --> 00:09:23.942
So what builds the ultimate volcano?

00:09:23.992 --> 00:09:24.729
Deep underground,

00:09:24.779 --> 00:09:26.490
molten rock called magma gathers

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.870
in a deep chamber fed by the mantle.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.051
It rises into a shallower chamber

00:09:31.101 --> 00:09:32.704
just beneath the surface.

00:09:32.754 --> 00:09:33.622
From there,

00:09:33.672 --> 00:09:36.952
heat and pressure force it up a main pipe,

00:09:37.002 --> 00:09:37.507
the conduit,

00:09:37.557 --> 00:09:39.792
toward the summit vent and crater.

00:09:39.842 --> 00:09:40.671
Sometimes it breaks

00:09:40.721 --> 00:09:43.212
out sideways through cracks in the flank,

00:09:43.262 --> 00:09:44.403
called side vents.

00:09:45.153 --> 00:09:46.220
When the pressure

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:47.797
finally becomes too much

00:09:47.847 --> 00:09:48.809
for the crust to hold,

00:09:48.859 --> 00:09:51.363
the energy is released all at once.

00:09:51.413 --> 00:09:52.823
In 1980, Mount St.

00:09:52.873 --> 00:09:55.091
Helens in the United States exploded

00:09:55.141 --> 00:09:55.880
so violently

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.693
that its whole north side collapsed,

00:09:58.743 --> 00:10:00.743
and the mountain lost about 400 metres

00:10:00.793 --> 00:10:02.714
of its height in a single day.

00:10:03.464 --> 00:10:05.640
Rock that has melted into a glowing,

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:08.224
semi-liquid state is called lava.

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:09.306
How fast it flows depends

00:10:09.356 --> 00:10:10.402
on its temperature,

00:10:10.452 --> 00:10:13.004
its minerals and the slope of the ground.

00:10:13.054 --> 00:10:15.225
The fastest lava ever measured poured

00:10:15.275 --> 00:10:16.215
from Nyiragongo,

00:10:16.265 --> 00:10:18.688
in the Democratic Republic of the Congo,

00:10:18.738 --> 00:10:20.954
at more than 60 kilometres per hour.

00:10:21.004 --> 00:10:22.646
When lava reaches the ocean,

00:10:22.696 --> 00:10:25.305
it boils the seawater into clouds of steam.

00:10:26.055 --> 00:10:27.291
Some eruptions are

00:10:27.341 --> 00:10:29.455
almost beyond imagination.

00:10:29.505 --> 00:10:31.545
Nearly two thousand years ago, a volcano

00:10:31.595 --> 00:10:33.576
in New Zealand blasted

00:10:33.626 --> 00:10:35.719
out about 100 cubic kilometres

00:10:35.769 --> 00:10:37.576
of rock in one of the most

00:10:37.626 --> 00:10:39.755
violent eruptions ever recorded.

00:10:39.805 --> 00:10:43.325
The giant crater it left behind is now filled

00:10:43.375 --> 00:10:43.875
with water.

00:10:43.925 --> 00:10:45.576
Today, it is Lake Taupo.

00:10:46.326 --> 00:10:49.466
Ash can be just as deadly as lava.

00:10:49.516 --> 00:10:50.016
In AD 79,

00:10:50.066 --> 00:10:53.146
Mount Vesuvius in Italy shot a column

00:10:53.196 --> 00:10:55.808
of ash and pumice 33 kilometres

00:10:55.858 --> 00:10:57.783
into the sky, at a rate

00:10:57.833 --> 00:11:01.303
of about 1.5 million tonnes every second.

00:11:01.353 --> 00:11:03.536
When the column collapsed,

00:11:03.586 --> 00:11:06.756
it buried the nearby Roman cities,

00:11:06.806 --> 00:11:08.001
including Pompeii,

00:11:08.051 --> 00:11:11.007
under a suffocating blanket of ash.

00:11:11.757 --> 00:11:14.077
Every eruption leaves its mark.

00:11:14.127 --> 00:11:16.467
The crater is the opening of the vent.

00:11:16.517 --> 00:11:18.055
Layer after layer of lava

00:11:18.105 --> 00:11:20.227
and ash builds up around it,

00:11:20.277 --> 00:11:22.427
creating the classic cone shape.

00:11:22.477 --> 00:11:25.507
If the walls collapse, the crater grows wider.

00:11:25.557 --> 00:11:27.377
On the islands of Hawaii,

00:11:27.427 --> 00:11:29.777
some of the most active volcanoes

00:11:29.827 --> 00:11:31.195
on Earth breathe steam and gas

00:11:31.245 --> 00:11:33.257
from their craters almost every day,

00:11:33.307 --> 00:11:35.278
slowly building new land.

00:11:37.128 --> 00:11:38.244
In some places,

00:11:38.294 --> 00:11:41.388
Earth's inner heat does not stay hidden.

00:11:41.438 --> 00:11:43.144
It rises to the surface

00:11:43.194 --> 00:11:45.438
and bakes the rock underfoot.

00:11:45.488 --> 00:11:47.733
Water trapped in the ground is

00:11:47.783 --> 00:11:49.878
heated far past boiling point,

00:11:49.928 --> 00:11:53.718
until it bursts out as jets of scalding steam.

00:11:53.768 --> 00:11:55.418
In the hottest places,

00:11:55.468 --> 00:11:57.689
even the mud begins to bubble.

00:11:58.439 --> 00:11:59.329
The most famous

00:11:59.379 --> 00:12:01.579
of these jets is Old Faithful,

00:12:01.629 --> 00:12:02.963
in Yellowstone National Park

00:12:03.013 --> 00:12:04.709
in the United States.

00:12:04.759 --> 00:12:06.284
It shoots hot water and steam up

00:12:06.334 --> 00:12:07.913
to 55 metres into the air,

00:12:07.963 --> 00:12:09.793
roughly every hour and a half.

00:12:09.843 --> 00:12:10.801
The wait is not random:

00:12:10.851 --> 00:12:12.259
that is how long it takes

00:12:12.309 --> 00:12:14.241
for the chamber beneath

00:12:14.291 --> 00:12:16.560
to fill with water and heat up again.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:20.110
Yellowstone is a land of extremes.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:21.123
It holds around half

00:12:21.173 --> 00:12:23.160
of all the geysers on Earth,

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:25.701
along with thousands of hot springs,

00:12:25.751 --> 00:12:27.320
mud pots and steaming vents.

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:29.134
But all of this sits

00:12:29.184 --> 00:12:31.230
on top of something far more powerful:

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:32.850
a supervolcano.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:34.428
The whole region lies

00:12:34.478 --> 00:12:36.420
inside an enormous caldera,

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:38.260
a collapsed volcanic crater tens

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.151
of kilometres across.

00:12:40.901 --> 00:12:43.521
Ordinary volcanoes build mountains.

00:12:43.571 --> 00:12:46.321
Supervolcanoes blow them apart.

00:12:46.371 --> 00:12:48.006
Yellowstone's last giant eruption,

00:12:48.056 --> 00:12:49.632
about 640,000 years ago,

00:12:49.682 --> 00:12:51.061
was roughly a thousand times

00:12:51.111 --> 00:12:54.165
more powerful than the 1980 eruption

00:12:54.215 --> 00:12:55.015
of Mount St.

00:12:55.065 --> 00:12:55.565
Helens.

00:12:55.615 --> 00:12:58.060
Underground, the water still reaches

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.185
more than 200 degrees Celsius,

00:13:00.235 --> 00:13:02.026
and new geysers, mud pools

00:13:02.076 --> 00:13:04.222
and steam vents keep appearing.

00:13:04.972 --> 00:13:07.571
The tallest geyser ever seen lived

00:13:07.621 --> 00:13:09.542
for only four years.

00:13:09.592 --> 00:13:12.325
After a volcanic eruption in New Zealand,

00:13:12.375 --> 00:13:15.552
the Waimangu Geyser began erupting

00:13:15.602 --> 00:13:16.102
in 1900.

00:13:16.152 --> 00:13:17.468
Its jets of black water,

00:13:17.518 --> 00:13:21.272
rock and steam rose as high as 460 metres,

00:13:21.322 --> 00:13:23.492
taller than the Empire State Building.

00:13:23.542 --> 00:13:25.105
In 1903, a sudden,

00:13:25.155 --> 00:13:27.599
violent eruption swept away four

00:13:27.649 --> 00:13:30.093
tourists who had come too close.

00:13:30.843 --> 00:13:33.113
Not every hot spring is dangerous.

00:13:33.163 --> 00:13:35.393
In the mountains of Nagano, Japan,

00:13:35.443 --> 00:13:36.019
warm pools fed

00:13:36.069 --> 00:13:38.058
by hot springs have been adopted

00:13:38.108 --> 00:13:40.283
by an unlikely set of bathers:

00:13:40.333 --> 00:13:42.263
Japanese macaque monkeys.

00:13:42.313 --> 00:13:43.943
Through the freezing winter,

00:13:43.993 --> 00:13:46.163
they soak in these natural hot tubs,

00:13:46.213 --> 00:13:46.713
and some get

00:13:46.763 --> 00:13:49.084
so comfortable they doze off in the water.

00:13:49.834 --> 00:13:51.864
And at Pamukkale in Turkey,

00:13:51.914 --> 00:13:53.085
mineral-rich hot water

00:13:53.135 --> 00:13:54.954
pours down a hillside.

00:13:55.004 --> 00:13:56.344
As the water cools,

00:13:56.394 --> 00:13:58.144
its minerals turn to stone,

00:13:58.194 --> 00:14:00.295
building gleaming white terraces filled

00:14:00.345 --> 00:14:02.144
with warm blue pools.

00:14:02.194 --> 00:14:04.314
Its name means "Cotton Castle",

00:14:04.364 --> 00:14:04.927
and it is one

00:14:04.977 --> 00:14:07.235
of the most beautiful places on Earth.

00:14:09.084 --> 00:14:12.365
Rock is tough, but it is also brittle.

00:14:12.414 --> 00:14:13.763
Under the constant stirring

00:14:13.813 --> 00:14:15.145
of the Earth below,

00:14:15.195 --> 00:14:16.454
the rocky crust is

00:14:16.504 --> 00:14:18.794
pushed past its breaking point.

00:14:18.844 --> 00:14:20.382
When crunch time comes,

00:14:20.432 --> 00:14:23.475
giant blocks of rock buckle and slip,

00:14:23.524 --> 00:14:24.238
and the surface

00:14:24.288 --> 00:14:26.565
of the Earth shakes and shatters.

00:14:27.315 --> 00:14:29.265
It happens along faults:

00:14:29.315 --> 00:14:30.724
enormous cracks in the crust

00:14:30.774 --> 00:14:31.788
where two blocks

00:14:31.838 --> 00:14:34.105
of rock move in different directions.

00:14:34.155 --> 00:14:36.245
As they grind past each other,

00:14:36.295 --> 00:14:37.375
they can get stuck.

00:14:37.425 --> 00:14:39.905
Pressure builds up, year after year,

00:14:39.955 --> 00:14:42.098
until suddenly the rock gives way

00:14:42.148 --> 00:14:43.465
and jolts forward.

00:14:43.515 --> 00:14:45.756
That sudden release is an earthquake.

00:14:46.506 --> 00:14:47.958
The place deep underground

00:14:48.008 --> 00:14:49.113
where the rock first

00:14:49.163 --> 00:14:50.806
breaks is called the focus.

00:14:50.856 --> 00:14:52.578
The point on the surface directly

00:14:52.628 --> 00:14:54.006
above it is the epicentre.

00:14:54.056 --> 00:14:54.946
From the focus,

00:14:54.996 --> 00:14:56.447
seismic waves race outward

00:14:56.497 --> 00:14:57.826
in every direction,

00:14:57.876 --> 00:14:58.584
shaking the ground

00:14:58.634 --> 00:14:59.912
and causing destruction

00:14:59.962 --> 00:15:01.067
in towns and cities.

00:15:01.817 --> 00:15:04.097
Some earthquakes have changed history.

00:15:04.147 --> 00:15:07.877
In 1906, movement along the San Andreas

00:15:07.927 --> 00:15:09.637
Fault tore through San Francisco,

00:15:09.687 --> 00:15:11.417
destroying much of the city.

00:15:11.467 --> 00:15:12.314
And in 2010,

00:15:12.364 --> 00:15:15.557
an earthquake in Chile was so powerful

00:15:15.607 --> 00:15:17.165
that it shifted the entire city

00:15:17.215 --> 00:15:19.752
of Concepción about three metres

00:15:19.802 --> 00:15:20.258
to the west.

00:15:21.008 --> 00:15:23.145
In 2008, a massive earthquake

00:15:23.195 --> 00:15:24.879
struck Sichuan Province

00:15:24.929 --> 00:15:26.528
in southwest China.

00:15:26.578 --> 00:15:29.848
In Beichuan County, roads split apart,

00:15:29.898 --> 00:15:30.851
hillsides collapsed

00:15:30.901 --> 00:15:33.828
and whole towns were reduced to rubble,

00:15:33.878 --> 00:15:34.832
forcing survivors

00:15:34.882 --> 00:15:37.169
to walk out along broken roads.

00:15:37.919 --> 00:15:39.213
Earthquakes are among

00:15:39.263 --> 00:15:41.159
the deadliest natural events on Earth,

00:15:41.209 --> 00:15:43.054
and predicting exactly

00:15:43.104 --> 00:15:44.334
when one will strike

00:15:44.384 --> 00:15:46.254
is still virtually impossible.

00:15:46.304 --> 00:15:48.099
What we can do is build smarter.

00:15:48.149 --> 00:15:50.059
In Japan and California,

00:15:50.109 --> 00:15:51.887
buildings use flexible materials

00:15:51.937 --> 00:15:53.871
and shock-absorbing foundations

00:15:53.921 --> 00:15:56.439
that let them sway instead of collapse.

00:15:56.489 --> 00:15:58.800
Scientists even demolish old buildings

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:01.168
on purpose to test steel safety cages

00:16:01.218 --> 00:16:03.280
that could protect people inside.

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:06.650
When a fault slips beneath the sea,

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:08.770
the whole ocean can move.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:09.880
A sudden jolt

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:13.730
of the seafloor pushes up a huge volume

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:15.941
of water and sends it racing outward

00:16:15.991 --> 00:16:16.850
as a tsunami.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:19.480
When the fault lies close to land,

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:22.150
the waves can be devastating.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:24.427
In 2004, a mega-tsunami swept

00:16:24.477 --> 00:16:26.860
across the Indian Ocean,

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:28.145
devastating coastlines

00:16:28.195 --> 00:16:30.690
from Indonesia to Thailand,

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:32.751
including the Phi Phi Islands.
